# BUILD FLOW 01.02 — .gitignore
# First introduced: Phase 01
# Purpose: List the files Git must never track: secrets, build output, downloaded
#          dependencies, logs and local data, and editor or operating-system clutter.
# Depends on: Nothing. Git reads this file before `git status` and `git add`.
# Used by: Git, on every status/add/commit. Later phases may append sections.
# Runtime role: None at application runtime; it protects every future commit.
#
# Syntax: one pattern per line. "name/" matches a directory with that name at any
# depth. "*" is a wildcard. "!" re-includes something an earlier line excluded.
# Ignoring only affects untracked files: it cannot hide a file that is already committed,
# which is why these rules exist before the first commit.

# BUILD FLOW 01.02.01 — Secrets and machine-specific configuration
# Real .env files hold passwords. Only the placeholder template (.env.example) is committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# Private keys and certificates (for example an EC2 SSH key) must never enter Git history.
*.pem
*.key
*.ppk
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*

# BUILD FLOW 01.02.02 — Build output (recreated by the build tools)
# Maven writes to target/, Vite to dist/, and test runners to coverage/.
target/
dist/
coverage/

# BUILD FLOW 01.02.03 — Downloaded dependencies (recreated from lockfiles)
node_modules/

# BUILD FLOW 01.02.04 — Logs and local data
# Logs and database backups can contain real data, so they stay out of Git.
*.log
logs/
backups/

# BUILD FLOW 01.02.05 — Editors, IDEs, and operating systems
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# BUILD FLOW 01.02.06 — Java editor metadata and wrapper binaries (added in Phase 02)
# Eclipse/STS and the VS Code Java extension can create these files; IntelliJ creates *.iws and
# *.ipr. They describe one person's editor setup, not the project, so they stay local.
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath
.apt_generated/
.springBeans
.sts4-cache/
*.iws
*.ipr
# Our Maven Wrapper runs in "only-script" mode, which needs no jar. If a wrapper jar ever
# appears, keep it out. The **/ prefix matches it inside backend/ too: a pattern with a slash
# in the middle would otherwise only match relative to this file's own folder.
**/.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
